    "done_when": "With JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu, tests/test_contour_tree_brute_force.py runs the brute-force comparison on (a) the four Solov'ev certificates at their persisted rung and at the 340, 550, 1074 and 2616-cell rungs where those persisted states exist (name any rung that does not exist rather than substituting), and (b) MAST 27079 row 16 and 22475 row 50 on the full 464-cell carrier (644 vertices), each with tree and brute-force component counts equal at every sampled level, node count minus edge count one, and overflow false. A focused test in tests/test_contour_tree_merge.py shows a mesh with more vertices than the node capacity but few critical points builds without overflow, and that a field with more critical points than the node capacity sets the named overflow flag without raising under jit and without truncating silently. vmap and per-state trees stay identical. Record compile seconds and execute wall per rung on CPU. The other contour-tree tests (merge, mesh, conventions) still pass. Negative control: restore the vertex-count overflow condition and show the 464-cell MAST and 340-cell certificate cases fail with overflow; commit that log. Logs and per-rung figures (data-ink, component count against level, tree over brute force) under docs/figures/contour-tree-topology-authority/capacity-on-critical-nodes/, plus a short evidence fragment with the table. Record baseline_suite and after_suite as completed arms. Run the gates in place on this fleet allocation, logs written once to files; pytest --timeout 0. Every uv command carries --no-sync and nothing runs uv sync. The manifest status is one of complete, blocked or failed.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Make build_contour_tree (nova/equilibrium/contour_tree.py) accept production-resolution hex carriers. Today it sets overflow whenever the mesh vertex count exceeds its 256-node capacity (the vertex_count > node_capacity comparison at the top of the sweep), although the trees it builds need only 13 to 52 nodes; every certificate rung from 340 cells up and the full 464-cell MAST carrier are refused, and the brute-force control had to run MAST at 90 cells (record: docs/evidence/archive/contour-tree-topology-authority-landed.html#end-to-end-brute-force). Judge overflow on what the receipt actually stores: critical nodes and tree edges against their stated capacities, with the sweep's working arrays sized by the mesh's own vertex and edge capacities. Plan rules locked today by the plan session: every refusal inside the traced read (capacity overflow, primary tie, type or order disagreement) is a named receipt flag marking the topology unqualified, and nothing raises under jit or vmap; nodes store raw psi and critical_type is classified on raw psi (under sigma=-1 the axis is critical_type 0); the carrier is the lattice of hex-cell centres; the axis is the primary O-point.",
